  "fresh_brief": "A reader of the Lab's examples wants to check downloaded evidence against a supplied inventory. Implement the library in examples/evidence_files.py and the command in examples/check_evidence.py. Only those two files may change.\n\ncheck_files(manifest) takes a pathlib.Path to a UTF-8 JSON file mapping relative POSIX filenames to lowercase SHA-256 hex digests. The manifest and its schema are trusted and valid; paths are distinct ordinary relative paths with no symlinks or traversal. Resolve them relative to the manifest's parent. Each target is either a regular file or absent. Hash exact bytes. Return a list of (filename, status) tuples sorted by filename, including every entry. Status is \"ok\", \"missing\" or \"changed\". An empty manifest yields an empty list. The function must not print, write files, or change the current directory.\n\nRun python examples/check_evidence.py MANIFEST from any working directory (use the script's absolute path when outside the repository). On stdout emit one JSON object with keys \"ok\", \"missing\" and \"changed\", each containing the sorted list of filenames in that category, including empty lists. Exit 0 if all entries are ok (including an empty manifest), otherwise 1. Both outcomes are ordinary reports, with empty stderr. Unlisted files are irrelevant. The reader supplies exactly one valid manifest argument. Stream formatting and JSON key order are flexible.\n\nAn example inventory and its recorded evidence are in examples/reviewing-worker-output. The public suite runs offline with python -m unittest discover -s tests.\n",
